ACC-003 — Core Principles

Document ID: ACC-003
Title: Core Principles
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification establishes the fundamental principles that govern the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

These principles define the architectural philosophy of Atlas and provide the normative foundation for all concepts, specifications, and implementations.

2. Scope

The Core Principles apply to:

Atlas Core Concepts (ACC)
Atlas Hivemind Specification (AHS)
Engineering Understanding Specification (EUS)
Engineering Memory Specification (EMS)
Engineering Knowledge Specification (EKS)
Engineering Evidence Specification (EES)
Engineering Reasoning Specification (ERS)
Engineering Capability Specification (ECS)
Decision Support Specification (DSS)

All Atlas specifications SHALL conform to these principles.

3. Core Principles
3.1 Reality-Oriented

Atlas SHALL model engineering reality rather than documentation, software artifacts, or data alone.

All engineering cognition SHALL ultimately correspond to the real-world engineering domain.

3.2 Evidence-Based

Engineering conclusions SHALL be derived from verifiable engineering evidence.

Assertions without supporting evidence SHALL NOT be treated as established understanding.

3.3 Context-Aware

Engineering concepts SHALL be interpreted within their applicable engineering context.

Context SHALL be considered an essential component of engineering cognition.

3.4 Explainable

Every engineering conclusion SHALL be explainable through traceable reasoning and supporting evidence.

Atlas SHALL prioritize transparency over opaque decision-making.

3.5 Traceable

Engineering cognition SHALL maintain traceability between:

Reality
Observation
Evidence
Understanding
Reasoning
Memory
Knowledge
3.6 Consistent

Engineering concepts SHALL maintain consistent meaning across all Atlas specifications.

Conflicting interpretations SHALL NOT exist within the framework.

3.7 Evolvable

Engineering cognition SHALL evolve as new observations, evidence, and engineering knowledge become available.

Atlas SHALL support continuous refinement without compromising conceptual integrity.

3.8 Technology Independent

The Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework SHALL remain independent of implementation technologies.

Its concepts SHALL apply regardless of programming language, AI model, database, hardware platform, or software architecture.

3.9 Domain Independent

Atlas SHALL provide a generic engineering cognition framework applicable across engineering disciplines.

Domain-specific specialization SHALL extend, but SHALL NOT redefine, the Atlas Core Concepts.

3.10 Human-Centric

Atlas SHALL support and augment engineering decision-making.

Final engineering authority SHALL remain with qualified human engineers unless explicitly delegated within a defined operational context.

4. Normative Requirements

All Atlas specifications SHALL:

Conform to the Core Principles defined in this specification.
Preserve conceptual consistency.
Maintain traceability across engineering cognition.
Avoid technology-specific assumptions.
Support explainability and evidence-based reasoning.
5. Conformance

A specification conforms to ACC-003 if it:

Implements the Core Principles defined herein.
Does not contradict any principle.
Maintains consistency with other Atlas specifications.
Preserves the integrity of the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework.
6. Non-Goals

This specification does not:

Define engineering methods.
Specify implementation architecture.
Mandate software technologies.
Replace engineering standards or regulations.
Define operational procedures.
7. Future Evolution

The Core Principles are intended to remain stable throughout the evolution of the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework.

Future revisions MAY introduce additional principles provided they do not invalidate or contradict the existing principles.